{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 claims O(n^3) time 'polynomial in n alone,' but its described algorithm computes 'optimal coin counts via dynamic programming' for amounts in the intervals [c_i, c_{i-1}+c_i]. Building a DP table for optimal coin counts up to amount c_{i-1}+c_i requires time proportional to that magnitude — at least Omega(c_{i-1}+c_i) per interval — which depends on coin magnitudes, not on n alone. The amounts tested can be as large as c_1+c_2, so the overall running time of the described method is pseudo-polynomial, directly contradicting the target's own claim of a bound polynomial in n alone. The internal inconsistency between the stated complexity and the described algorithm is a genuine fault against requirement 3.","target":"5731f7379b0aa470275891668bb942b8bf98617671eee6511aa60e5366f3801b"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 fails requirement 1 (state the algorithm precisely enough to implement) and requirement 2 (prove correctness, justifying why a finite bounded set suffices). Its mechanism only vaguely says it examines 'structural differences' and 'combinations of up to three coins' without specifying which combinations, how candidate witnesses are generated from them, what condition is tested for each candidate, or how the minimal counterexample is identified and verified to be globally minimal. No proof is given that testing such combinations suffices to cover all possible counterexamples. The problem explicitly demands a precise, implementable algorithm and a correctness proof; the target provides neither.","target":"ceeb35d23ac2e2b1ec39c5ebc79758fd97e4cfe37d56731d21ed4ecb757b091f"}]}